<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Ventris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Ventris was an English architect and amateur linguist who deciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, the script of the Mycenaean palaces. In 1952, working from the tablets found at Pylos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, he showed that it recorded an early form of Greek, and with John Chadwick he published Documents in Mycenaean Greek in 1956. He was killed in a car accident the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-ventris.jpg" alt="Michael Ventris" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Ventris_obituary_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael Ventris was born on 12 July 1922 into an army family, the only child of a lieutenant-colonel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> and a mother from a wealthy Jewish and Polish family. The family spent eight years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he started school at Gstaad with classes in French and German, and he was fluent in Polish by the age of eight. He later learned Swedish and Russian.</p><p>At Stowe School he spent much of his spare time learning about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>. In 1936, aged 14, he heard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> lecture at an exhibition of Cretan archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and spoke up to confirm that Linear B was undeciphered; he determined to decipher it. In 1940, aged 18, he published an article in the American Journal of Archaeology, Introducing the Minoan Language, which supposed a relation between Etruscan and Linear B.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ventris studied architecture, was conscripted in 1942 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, qualified as a navigator in 1944 and flew in the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with No. 76 Squadron. After the war he completed his architectural education with honours in 1948 and designed schools for the Ministry of Education. His work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> built on Alice Kober's observation that some words had changing endings, like declensions.</p><p>He constructed grids that linked the symbols to consonants and vowels, and used photographs by Alison Frantz of some 600 tablets that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> had found at Pylos. Noticing that some groups of symbols appeared only in the Cretan texts, he guessed that they were place names, and this proved correct.</p><p>On 1 July 1952 he announced preliminary findings on the BBC, showing that the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> was Greek. John Chadwick, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> classicist, heard the broadcast and began to collaborate with him. In 1953 a tablet from Pylos showing a tripod cauldron beside symbols that Ventris and Chadwick read as ti-ri-po-de confirmed the reading. Their work showed that Cretan civilisation, at least in the periods of the Linear B tablets, had been part of Mycenaean Greece, which overturned Evans's theories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ventris was awarded an OBE in 1955 for services to Mycenaean palaeography. On 6 September 1956 he died at Hatfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, when his car struck a parked lorry, a few weeks before the publication of Documents in Mycenaean Greek; the coroner's verdict was accidental death. The British Academy gave him the Kenyon Medal posthumously in 1959. Some scholars remained sceptical of the decipherment into the 1960s. An English Heritage blue plaque marks his home in Hampstead, a street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a> is named after him, and the IAU named the Ventris crater on the far side of the Moon after him in 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Ventris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1952 the English architect <b>Michael Ventris</b> showed that the language of Linear B was Greek, more than seven centuries older than Homer.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Alice Kober showed in the 1940s that the language was inflected; <b>Michael Ventris</b>, an architect, announced in 1952 that it was an early form of Greek, and a tablet from Pylos with pictures of tripods and vessels, read in 1953, confirmed it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Vilhelm Thomsen read the Old Turkic runes in 1893, beginning with the words for “heaven” and “Turk”, and in 1952 <b>Michael Ventris</b> showed that Linear B was Greek.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>Its tablets, baked by the fire, were found by Carl Blegen in 1939 and helped <b>Michael Ventris</b> show in 1952 that Linear B was Greek.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pylos">Kingdom of Pylos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wheathampstead, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.81,-0.29&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Wheathampstead on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4543 372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michael Ventris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.81,-0.29&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
